  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$ and $f(-2)=2,$ then what is the value of\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}({(f(x))}^{2}+4)\\,?\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=f(-2)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}({(f(x))}^{2}+4) & =({2}^{2}+4)=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
